"home":true,"pf":21.0,"pa":17.0,"off_snaps":56,"epa_per_play":-0.1349,"pass_rate":0.4643,"pass_rate_neutral":0.5789,"def_epa_per_play":-0.1648},{"week":14,"opponent":"NYJ","home":false,"pf":34.0,"pa":10.0,"off_snaps":61,"epa_per_play":0.2068,"pass_rate":0.3607,"pass_rate_neutral":0.4444,"def_epa_per_play":-0.4528},{"week":15,"opponent":"PIT","home":false,"pf":15.0,"pa":28.0,"off_snaps":50,"epa_per_play":-0.04028,"pass_rate":0.68,"pass_rate_neutral":0.4,"def_epa_per_play":0.2232},{"week":16,"opponent":"CIN","home":true,"pf":21.0,"pa":45.0,"off_snaps":58,"epa_per_play":-0.05744,"pass_rate":0.5172,"pass_rate_neutral":0.4483,"def_epa_per_play":0.3722},{"week":17,"opponent":"TB","home":true,"pf":20.0,"pa":17.0,"off_snaps":52,"epa_per_play":0.1076,"pass_rate":0.4615,"pass_rate_neutral":0.5161,"def_epa_per_play":0.03507},{"week":18,"opponent":"NE","home":false,"pf":10.0,"pa":38.0,"off_snaps":54,"epa_per_play":-0.3441,"pass_rate":0.537,"pass_rate_neutral":0.48,"def_epa_per_play":0.3599}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2022,"team":"SF","distance":1.153,"shared":21,"play_caller":"Kyle Shanahan"},{"season":2023,"team":"BAL","distance":1.254,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Todd Monken"},{"season":2025,"team":"LAC","distance":1.266,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Greg Roman"},{"season":2023,"team":"SF","distance":1.338,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Kyle Shanahan"},{"season":2023,"team":"CHI","distance":1.379,"shared":22,"play_caller":null}],"defense":[{"season":2019,"team":"NYJ","distance":0.7233,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2025,"team":"NE","distance":0.7861,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2021,"team":"JAX","distance":0.7974,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2025,"team":"PIT","distance":0.8078,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2025,"team":"TEN","distance":0.8434,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Dennard Wilson"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Dolphins went 7-10 in 2025 and were outscored 424 to 347. The offense ranked 22nd in EPA per play and the defense 26th in EPA allowed, so neither side stood out much. Their most distinctive traits were the highest 10 personnel usage and the 2nd highest Cover 2 rate in the league.","offense":"The Dolphins offense was a little below average by EPA per play, and it was much better running the ball than passing it. They were one of the most run-heavy teams in the league, passing less often than game situations would predict. Schematically, they stood out for the highest 10 personnel usage and the 2nd highest 21 personnel usage in the league. Tua Tagovailoa ran the passing game, with Jaylen Waddle leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Dolphins had one of the worst defenses in the league, ranking 25th or worse in four of the five main efficiency measures. Even its strongest area, sack rate, only ranked 16th, and it was 30th in success rate allowed. Zach Sieler was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Bradley Chubb, who had 8.5 sacks, after not being a regular on this defense last season. In terms of scheme, they had the 2nd highest Cover 2 rate in the league.","special":"The Dolphins were one of the most conservative teams on fourth down, going for it on only 18% of their short-yardage chances. Their kickers made 93% of field goals, which was one of the best in the league. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into receivers, about 19% of the money on the books. Their defensive line was among the youngest in the league.","players":"Tua Tagovailoa played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 2,660 yards. Targets were spread around, with Jaylen Waddle and De'Von Achane leading a group where nobody took more than a fifth of the looks. De'Von Achane handled most of the work in the backfield with 59% of the carries. On defense, Bradley Chubb led the team with 8.5 sacks and Rasul Douglas picked off 2 passes.","coaching":"Mike McDaniel called the offense, and the defensive play caller for this season is not in the coaching data. Their offense looked most like the 2022 49ers under Kyle Shanahan and the 2023 Ravens under Todd Monken. Their defense looked most like the 2019 Jets and the 2025 Patriots.","trends":"The offense went from 21st in EPA per play in 2024 to 22nd in 2025, about where it was. The defense moved from 9th in EPA allowed per play in 2024 to 26th in 2025, a big step back. The biggest change in approach was Cover 4 rate, which went from 11% to 7%.","notes":"Every data source is available for 2025. This season uses the newer formation labels, which group singleback, I formation and empty sets together as under center, so those older formation rates are not shown."}}